Now let me understand the actual expected behavior. The issue title says "Go back from Flag as offensive doesn't display correct report page". The user reloads the flag page URL directly, then presses back. Since the page was reloaded, there's no navigation history to the report, and the back button resets to home instead of showing the report.

The fix likely: use the `backTo` param. When `hasPageReloaded`, instead of `Navigation.resetToHome()`, navigate to the `backTo` route.

But wait—on reload, `backTo` param is preserved in the URL. Let me verify how the URL is formed and parsed. In ContextMenuActions, activeRoute = Navigation.getActiveRoute(). For a report page, that would be `/r/8292963527436014` (or with `?` params). Then the flag route becomes `flag/8292963527436014/533025741267772385?backTo=%2Fr%2F8292963527436014`.

On reload, the linking config parses backTo into route params. So `route.params.backTo` = '/r/8292963527436014'.

So the fix: in handleBackPress, when hasPageReloaded, do:
```
if (hasPageReloaded) {
    Navigation.goBack(backTo);
} else {
    Navigation.goBack();
}
```
But `goBack` with fallbackRoute only uses fallback when isFirstRouteInNavigator. After reload in RHP, the flag screen is the first route of the modal navigator. So goBack(backTo) would navigate to backTo. But wait, `goBack` checks `canGoBack()`. After reload, can we go back? If not, it returns early. Hmm.

